1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,696
In nineteen forty one,

2
00:00:01,696 --> 00:00:04,704
German soldiers met a Soviet tank their

3
00:00:04,704 --> 00:00:06,478
guns could barely hurt.

4
00:00:06,658 --> 00:00:08,991
Its armor was not especially thick.

5
00:00:09,171 --> 00:00:10,442
But it was tilted,

6
00:00:10,442 --> 00:00:12,560
slanted back at a steep angle,

7
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:15,383
and shells that should have gone through

8
00:00:15,383 --> 00:00:16,230
bounced off.

9
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:18,984
That tank was the T thirty four,

10
00:00:18,984 --> 00:00:22,202
and its sloped armor changed tank design

11
00:00:22,202 --> 00:00:23,167
for decades.

12
00:00:23,347 --> 00:00:26,134
Today, eleven parts of a tank,

13
00:00:26,134 --> 00:00:28,363
and why each one exists,

14
00:00:28,363 --> 00:00:30,685
starting with that angle.

15
00:00:31,035 --> 00:00:33,202
A tank is a balance of three

16
00:00:33,202 --> 00:00:35,446
things that fight each other.

17
00:00:35,626 --> 00:00:38,865
Protection, firepower and mobility.

18
00:00:39,045 --> 00:00:41,336
More armor means more weight,

19
00:00:41,336 --> 00:00:44,101
and more weight means slower speed,

20
00:00:44,101 --> 00:00:47,261
more fuel, and bridges that cannot carry

21
00:00:47,261 --> 00:00:47,498
it.

22
00:00:47,678 --> 00:00:49,539
So every part of a tank is

23
00:00:49,539 --> 00:00:52,260
a trick to get more protection without

24
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:53,692
carrying more steel.

25
00:00:54,041 --> 00:00:56,905
As always, every fact comes from a

26
00:00:56,905 --> 00:01:00,443
named source, military history references,

27
00:01:00,443 --> 00:01:03,728
army publications, and museum archives,

28
00:01:03,728 --> 00:01:06,255
all listed in the description.

29
00:01:06,435 --> 00:01:08,958
Let's start with the slanted plate on

30
00:01:08,958 --> 00:01:09,640
the front.

31
00:01:09,989 --> 00:01:12,241
Why is the front armor of a

32
00:01:12,241 --> 00:01:13,658
tank tilted back?

33
00:01:13,838 --> 00:01:16,410
Because a shell hitting a slanted plate

34
00:01:16,410 --> 00:01:18,587
has to travel through more steel.

35
00:01:18,767 --> 00:01:22,447
Imagine a plate tilted at sixty degrees.

36
00:01:22,627 --> 00:01:25,371
A shell flying straight at it crosses

37
00:01:25,371 --> 00:01:27,522
a path about twice as long as

38
00:01:27,522 --> 00:01:28,931
the plate is thick.

39
00:01:29,111 --> 00:01:31,977
So a plate forty five millimeters thick

40
00:01:31,977 --> 00:01:34,402
can act like one of about ninety,

41
00:01:34,402 --> 00:01:36,019
without weighing more.

42
00:01:36,369 --> 00:01:38,192
There is a second effect.

43
00:01:38,372 --> 00:01:40,547
A shell that hits at a steep

44
00:01:40,547 --> 00:01:43,111
angle may not bite into the steel

45
00:01:43,111 --> 00:01:43,655
at all.

46
00:01:43,835 --> 00:01:46,644
It can skid along the surface and

47
00:01:46,644 --> 00:01:49,965
fly off, which tankers call a ricochet.

48
00:01:50,145 --> 00:01:52,669
The T thirty four's front plate was

49
00:01:52,669 --> 00:01:55,482
slanted at sixty degrees from vertical,

50
00:01:55,482 --> 00:01:58,655
and many early German shells simply bounced.

51
00:01:59,004 --> 00:02:00,850
The Germans learned fast.

52
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:02,683
Their Panther tank,

53
00:02:02,683 --> 00:02:05,466
built from nineteen forty three,

54
00:02:05,466 --> 00:02:08,424
copied the idea with a thick front

55
00:02:08,424 --> 00:02:11,468
plate sloped at fifty five degrees.

56
00:02:11,648 --> 00:02:12,707
After the war,

57
00:02:12,707 --> 00:02:15,279
almost every tank in the world had

58
00:02:15,279 --> 00:02:16,489
a slanted front.

59
00:02:16,669 --> 00:02:19,131
The angle was free protection,

60
00:02:19,131 --> 00:02:22,086
paid for only with a little interior

61
00:02:22,086 --> 00:02:22,579
space.

62
00:02:22,928 --> 00:02:24,786
Why do some modern tanks,

63
00:02:24,786 --> 00:02:27,684
like later versions of the Leopard two,

64
00:02:27,684 --> 00:02:30,285
have pointed wedges on the front of

65
00:02:30,285 --> 00:02:31,102
the turret?

66
00:02:31,282 --> 00:02:33,212
Because they are extra armor,

67
00:02:33,212 --> 00:02:35,475
mounted in front of the main armor

68
00:02:35,475 --> 00:02:37,006
with a gap behind them.

69
00:02:37,186 --> 00:02:39,957
A shell or missile hits the wedge

70
00:02:39,957 --> 00:02:41,972
first, at a steep angle,

71
00:02:41,972 --> 00:02:44,576
and part of its energy is spent

72
00:02:44,576 --> 00:02:47,851
or deflected before it reaches the real

73
00:02:47,851 --> 00:02:48,354
armor.

74
00:02:48,704 --> 00:02:50,971
Modern tank shells are long,

75
00:02:50,971 --> 00:02:53,481
thin darts of very dense metal,

76
00:02:53,481 --> 00:02:56,233
fired at more than a kilometer and

77
00:02:56,233 --> 00:02:57,691
a half per second.

78
00:02:57,871 --> 00:03:00,973
Against these, slope helps less than it

79
00:03:00,973 --> 00:03:03,200
did in the nineteen forties,

80
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,824
because a dart tends to turn into

81
00:03:05,824 --> 00:03:08,529
the plate instead of bouncing off.

82
00:03:08,709 --> 00:03:12,834
So designers combine slope with spaced layers

83
00:03:12,834 --> 00:03:14,850
and special materials.

84
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:18,103
The wedge is also a modular part.

85
00:03:18,283 --> 00:03:20,428
When better armor is developed,

86
00:03:20,428 --> 00:03:22,989
the wedge can be removed and replaced

87
00:03:22,989 --> 00:03:25,342
without rebuilding the whole tank.

88
00:03:25,522 --> 00:03:28,999
Modern armor is designed to be upgraded,

89
00:03:28,999 --> 00:03:32,128
like software, one module at a time.

90
00:03:32,478 --> 00:03:34,787
Why can no one tell you exactly

91
00:03:34,787 --> 00:03:36,947
what is inside the armor of a

92
00:03:36,947 --> 00:03:37,841
modern tank?

93
00:03:38,021 --> 00:03:40,274
Because it is not just steel.

94
00:03:40,454 --> 00:03:43,380
In the nineteen sixties and seventies,

95
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:46,691
British researchers developed a new kind of

96
00:03:46,691 --> 00:03:49,463
armor, later known as Chobham armor.

97
00:03:49,643 --> 00:03:52,828
It uses layers of different materials,

98
00:03:52,828 --> 00:03:55,175
such as ceramics and metals,

99
00:03:55,175 --> 00:03:56,516
packed together.

100
00:03:56,696 --> 00:03:59,600
Each layer breaks or slows the attacker

101
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,015
in a different way.

102
00:04:01,364 --> 00:04:03,385
Ceramics are very hard.

103
00:04:03,565 --> 00:04:05,977
They shatter the tip of an incoming

104
00:04:05,977 --> 00:04:07,907
dart or disrupt the jet of a

105
00:04:07,907 --> 00:04:09,010
missile warhead.

106
00:04:09,190 --> 00:04:12,371
The metal layers behind catch the fragments.

107
00:04:12,551 --> 00:04:15,374
Together, they stop much more than the

108
00:04:15,374 --> 00:04:17,381
same weight of plain steel.

109
00:04:17,561 --> 00:04:20,281
The American M one Abrams and the

110
00:04:20,281 --> 00:04:24,155
British Challenger were built around this idea.

111
00:04:24,504 --> 00:04:27,848
The exact recipe is still classified.

112
00:04:28,028 --> 00:04:31,494
Later versions of the Abrams were reported

113
00:04:31,494 --> 00:04:33,475
to add depleted uranium,

114
00:04:33,475 --> 00:04:35,043
a very dense metal,

115
00:04:35,043 --> 00:04:36,445
inside the armor.

116
00:04:36,625 --> 00:04:39,891
The boxy, flat-sided look of modern tanks

117
00:04:39,891 --> 00:04:41,165
comes from this.

118
00:04:41,345 --> 00:04:45,030
Composite armor works best in thick flat

119
00:04:45,030 --> 00:04:48,070
packs, so the turret became a big

120
00:04:48,070 --> 00:04:48,439
box.

121
00:04:48,788 --> 00:04:52,032
Why are some tanks covered in boxes

122
00:04:52,032 --> 00:04:53,699
full of explosive?

123
00:04:53,879 --> 00:04:56,958
Because these boxes destroy the weapon before

124
00:04:56,958 --> 00:04:58,326
it reaches the tank.

125
00:04:58,506 --> 00:05:01,548
They are called explosive reactive armor.

126
00:05:01,728 --> 00:05:04,607
Many anti tank missiles use a shaped

127
00:05:04,607 --> 00:05:07,566
charge, which turns a metal cone into

128
00:05:07,566 --> 00:05:10,125
a jet of metal moving at several

129
00:05:10,125 --> 00:05:11,884
kilometers per second,

130
00:05:11,884 --> 00:05:14,603
able to punch through thick steel.

131
00:05:14,953 --> 00:05:17,956
When that jet hits a reactive armor

132
00:05:17,956 --> 00:05:21,388
brick, the explosive inside goes off and

133
00:05:21,388 --> 00:05:23,876
throws a steel plate outward,

134
00:05:23,876 --> 00:05:26,192
across the path of the jet.

135
00:05:26,372 --> 00:05:29,196
The moving plate keeps cutting into the

136
00:05:29,196 --> 00:05:30,717
jet and breaks it up,

137
00:05:30,717 --> 00:05:32,890
so much less of it reaches the

138
00:05:32,890 --> 00:05:33,686
main armor.

139
00:05:33,866 --> 00:05:36,591
Israel first used it in combat in

140
00:05:36,591 --> 00:05:39,150
Lebanon in nineteen eighty two,

141
00:05:39,150 --> 00:05:42,040
and the Soviet Union soon followed.

142
00:05:42,389 --> 00:05:43,968
It comes with a cost.

143
00:05:44,148 --> 00:05:46,622
A brick that explodes is gone,

144
00:05:46,622 --> 00:05:47,777
leaving a gap,

145
00:05:47,777 --> 00:05:50,910
and the blast is dangerous to soldiers

146
00:05:50,910 --> 00:05:52,972
walking next to the tank.

147
00:05:53,152 --> 00:05:56,007
Newer types are also designed to work

148
00:05:56,007 --> 00:05:59,017
against the darts fired by enemy tanks,

149
00:05:59,017 --> 00:06:01,024
not only against missiles.

150
00:06:01,373 --> 00:06:03,873
Why do some armored vehicles have a

151
00:06:03,873 --> 00:06:06,087
cage of metal bars around them?

152
00:06:06,267 --> 00:06:08,903
Because it is a cheap shield against

153
00:06:08,903 --> 00:06:10,807
rocket propelled grenades.

154
00:06:10,987 --> 00:06:13,348
The bars are spaced so that the

155
00:06:13,348 --> 00:06:16,167
warhead of an RPG gets caught between

156
00:06:16,167 --> 00:06:16,548
them.

157
00:06:16,728 --> 00:06:19,238
Its nose can be crushed or its

158
00:06:19,238 --> 00:06:20,995
fuze short circuited,

159
00:06:20,995 --> 00:06:23,589
so it fails to go off properly,

160
00:06:23,589 --> 00:06:26,517
or it explodes far enough away from

161
00:06:26,517 --> 00:06:28,860
the armor to do less damage.

162
00:06:29,210 --> 00:06:32,403
Slat armor does not stop every hit.

163
00:06:32,583 --> 00:06:35,863
Studies and military reports suggest it defeats

164
00:06:35,863 --> 00:06:38,655
roughly half of the grenades that strike

165
00:06:38,655 --> 00:06:38,864
it.

166
00:06:39,044 --> 00:06:40,224
But it is light,

167
00:06:40,224 --> 00:06:42,289
cheap, and quick to bolt on,

168
00:06:42,289 --> 00:06:44,649
so armies used it widely in Iraq

169
00:06:44,649 --> 00:06:48,116
and Afghanistan, on trucks and armored vehicles

170
00:06:48,116 --> 00:06:50,550
that could not carry heavy armor.

171
00:06:50,899 --> 00:06:52,218
In recent wars,

172
00:06:52,218 --> 00:06:55,293
soldiers have added cages on top of

173
00:06:55,293 --> 00:06:59,511
turrets too, against drones that drop explosives

174
00:06:59,511 --> 00:07:00,478
from above.

175
00:07:00,658 --> 00:07:03,049
People sometimes laugh at them.

176
00:07:03,229 --> 00:07:05,443
But the logic is the same as

177
00:07:05,443 --> 00:07:08,605
the slanted plate in nineteen forty one.

178
00:07:08,785 --> 00:07:11,043
Find a cheap trick that makes the

179
00:07:11,043 --> 00:07:12,001
attacker fail.

180
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:14,569
Why does the M one Abrams have

181
00:07:14,569 --> 00:07:17,304
panels on its turret roof designed to

182
00:07:17,304 --> 00:07:17,970
blow off?

183
00:07:18,150 --> 00:07:20,767
Because the most dangerous thing in a

184
00:07:20,767 --> 00:07:22,678
tank is its own ammunition.

185
00:07:22,858 --> 00:07:24,980
If a hit sets the shells on

186
00:07:24,980 --> 00:07:27,339
fire, they can explode inside.

187
00:07:27,519 --> 00:07:28,652
On the Abrams,

188
00:07:28,652 --> 00:07:31,970
the main ammunition sits in a compartment

189
00:07:31,970 --> 00:07:34,074
at the back of the turret,

190
00:07:34,074 --> 00:07:36,421
behind sliding armored doors,

191
00:07:36,421 --> 00:07:38,363
separated from the crew.

192
00:07:38,712 --> 00:07:40,719
If that ammunition explodes,

193
00:07:40,719 --> 00:07:43,298
the blast takes the easiest way out.

194
00:07:43,478 --> 00:07:45,955
The roof panels above it are made

195
00:07:45,955 --> 00:07:47,306
weaker on purpose,

196
00:07:47,306 --> 00:07:49,482
so they blow off and send the

197
00:07:49,482 --> 00:07:51,433
explosion and fire upward,

198
00:07:51,433 --> 00:07:52,859
away from the crew.

199
00:07:53,039 --> 00:07:54,792
The tank may be destroyed,

200
00:07:54,792 --> 00:07:57,152
but the people inside have a chance

201
00:07:57,152 --> 00:07:58,299
to get out alive.

202
00:07:58,648 --> 00:08:01,859
Tanks that store ammunition around the crew,

203
00:08:01,859 --> 00:08:03,610
without this separation,

204
00:08:03,610 --> 00:08:05,069
have suffered badly.

205
00:08:05,249 --> 00:08:08,371
Photos from recent wars show turrets thrown

206
00:08:08,371 --> 00:08:11,204
meters into the air when the ammunition

207
00:08:11,204 --> 00:08:12,656
below them exploded.

208
00:08:12,836 --> 00:08:15,977
The blowout panel is a simple idea:

209
00:08:15,977 --> 00:08:18,310
if something must explode,

210
00:08:18,310 --> 00:08:19,477
choose where.

211
00:08:19,826 --> 00:08:22,577
Why does Israel's Merkava tank have its

212
00:08:22,577 --> 00:08:23,987
engine in the front,

213
00:08:23,987 --> 00:08:26,103
when most tanks have it at the

214
00:08:26,103 --> 00:08:26,456
back?

215
00:08:26,636 --> 00:08:29,118
Because the engine is a big block

216
00:08:29,118 --> 00:08:31,675
of steel that can stop shells too.

217
00:08:31,855 --> 00:08:36,075
Israel designed the Merkava with one priority

218
00:08:36,075 --> 00:08:39,076
above all, survival of the crew.

219
00:08:39,256 --> 00:08:41,830
Putting the engine in front means anything

220
00:08:41,830 --> 00:08:43,976
that hits the front must go through

221
00:08:43,976 --> 00:08:46,060
the engine before it can reach the

222
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:46,489
people.

223
00:08:46,839 --> 00:08:49,136
It also frees the back of the

224
00:08:49,136 --> 00:08:49,532
tank.

225
00:08:49,712 --> 00:08:51,873
The Merkava has a door at the

226
00:08:51,873 --> 00:08:53,959
rear, so the crew can get in

227
00:08:53,959 --> 00:08:55,450
and out under cover,

228
00:08:55,450 --> 00:08:57,015
away from enemy fire.

229
00:08:57,195 --> 00:09:00,722
That space can even carry wounded soldiers

230
00:09:00,722 --> 00:09:03,577
or extra infantry in an emergency,

231
00:09:03,577 --> 00:09:06,935
something few other battle tanks can do.

232
00:09:07,285 --> 00:09:09,756
The engine is not perfect armor,

233
00:09:09,756 --> 00:09:12,534
and a damaged engine leaves the tank

234
00:09:12,534 --> 00:09:12,998
stuck.

235
00:09:13,178 --> 00:09:15,019
But a tank can be repaired,

236
00:09:15,019 --> 00:09:17,269
and a trained crew is much harder

237
00:09:17,269 --> 00:09:18,019
to replace.

238
00:09:18,199 --> 00:09:19,301
In the Merkava,

239
00:09:19,301 --> 00:09:21,946
the whole layout of the machine says

240
00:09:21,946 --> 00:09:22,680
one thing.

241
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:24,381
The people matter most.

242
00:09:24,731 --> 00:09:27,207
Why do tank crews look out through

243
00:09:27,207 --> 00:09:29,828
small periscopes instead of windows?

244
00:09:30,008 --> 00:09:32,374
Because a window is a hole in

245
00:09:32,374 --> 00:09:33,189
the armor.

246
00:09:33,369 --> 00:09:36,412
Periscopes bend the light with mirrors or

247
00:09:36,412 --> 00:09:39,010
prisms, so the glass that the enemy

248
00:09:39,010 --> 00:09:40,420
can hit is outside,

249
00:09:40,420 --> 00:09:42,943
and the crew's eyes stay below the

250
00:09:42,943 --> 00:09:43,389
armor.

251
00:09:43,569 --> 00:09:45,838
If the outer glass is shattered,

252
00:09:45,838 --> 00:09:47,894
the periscope can be swapped,

253
00:09:47,894 --> 00:09:50,163
and no bullet comes straight in.

254
00:09:50,513 --> 00:09:52,879
The weakness is that a tank crew

255
00:09:52,879 --> 00:09:54,136
sees very little.

256
00:09:54,316 --> 00:09:56,740
Old tanks had narrow vision slits,

257
00:09:56,740 --> 00:09:59,592
and commanders often stood up with their

258
00:09:59,592 --> 00:10:01,589
head out of the hatch to see

259
00:10:01,589 --> 00:10:03,871
better, which made them targets.

260
00:10:04,051 --> 00:10:07,236
Modern tanks use thermal cameras and sights

261
00:10:07,236 --> 00:10:09,533
that show the world on screens,

262
00:10:09,533 --> 00:10:11,829
even at night or through smoke.

263
00:10:12,179 --> 00:10:14,936
Some new designs even use cameras around

264
00:10:14,936 --> 00:10:15,970
the whole tank,

265
00:10:15,970 --> 00:10:18,657
letting the crew look through the armor

266
00:10:18,657 --> 00:10:20,724
in any direction on a headset.

267
00:10:20,904 --> 00:10:23,521
But even the most modern tanks still

268
00:10:23,521 --> 00:10:25,920
keep simple periscopes as backup.

269
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:28,103
When the electronics fail,

270
00:10:28,103 --> 00:10:30,338
glass and mirrors still work.

271
00:10:30,687 --> 00:10:32,682
What are those small tubes on the

272
00:10:32,682 --> 00:10:34,194
sides of a tank's turret?

273
00:10:34,374 --> 00:10:36,754
They are smoke grenade launchers.

274
00:10:36,934 --> 00:10:39,061
When the crew sees a threat,

275
00:10:39,061 --> 00:10:41,416
like a missile being fired or a

276
00:10:41,416 --> 00:10:43,468
laser pointing at the tank,

277
00:10:43,468 --> 00:10:45,671
they can fire a wall of smoke

278
00:10:45,671 --> 00:10:48,102
in front of the tank in seconds.

279
00:10:48,282 --> 00:10:50,529
Then the tank can back away or

280
00:10:50,529 --> 00:10:52,776
move, hidden behind the cloud.

281
00:10:53,125 --> 00:10:56,366
Modern smoke does more than block normal

282
00:10:56,366 --> 00:10:56,852
sight.

283
00:10:57,032 --> 00:11:00,045
Some types also block infrared and thermal

284
00:11:00,045 --> 00:11:02,842
cameras, and the lasers that guide some

285
00:11:02,842 --> 00:11:03,487
missiles.

286
00:11:03,667 --> 00:11:06,811
Many tanks carry laser warning sensors that

287
00:11:06,811 --> 00:11:09,223
can fire the smoke automatically,

288
00:11:09,223 --> 00:11:11,562
faster than a human could react.

289
00:11:11,912 --> 00:11:14,284
It is the oldest form of defense

290
00:11:14,284 --> 00:11:15,692
on the battlefield,

291
00:11:15,692 --> 00:11:17,396
if they cannot see you,

292
00:11:17,396 --> 00:11:18,878
they cannot hit you,

293
00:11:18,878 --> 00:11:20,434
done in a modern way.

294
00:11:20,614 --> 00:11:23,703
A few kilograms of smoke can protect

295
00:11:23,703 --> 00:11:26,276
a tank of sixty tonnes as well

296
00:11:26,276 --> 00:11:27,992
as a meter of steel,

297
00:11:27,992 --> 00:11:30,308
for a few precious seconds.

298
00:11:30,658 --> 00:11:33,222
Why do tanks run on wide tracks

299
00:11:33,222 --> 00:11:34,710
instead of wheels?

300
00:11:34,890 --> 00:11:37,499
Because a tank weighs as much as

301
00:11:37,499 --> 00:11:40,598
forty cars, and wheels would sink into

302
00:11:40,598 --> 00:11:42,065
mud, sand or snow.

303
00:11:42,245 --> 00:11:44,691
A track spreads that weight over a

304
00:11:44,691 --> 00:11:46,634
long, wide strip of ground.

305
00:11:46,814 --> 00:11:49,602
A modern main battle tank presses on

306
00:11:49,602 --> 00:11:52,623
the ground with about the same pressure

307
00:11:52,623 --> 00:11:54,173
as a person walking,

308
00:11:54,173 --> 00:11:56,497
despite weighing sixty tonnes.

309
00:11:56,846 --> 00:11:59,545
The T thirty four had unusually wide

310
00:11:59,545 --> 00:12:01,044
tracks for its time,

311
00:12:01,044 --> 00:12:03,218
and in the mud of the Russian

312
00:12:03,218 --> 00:12:05,917
autumn, that gave it a big advantage

313
00:12:05,917 --> 00:12:08,166
over tanks with narrow tracks.

314
00:12:08,346 --> 00:12:10,844
Tracks also let a tank turn on

315
00:12:10,844 --> 00:12:13,840
the spot, by moving one side forward

316
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:16,171
and the other side backward.

317
00:12:16,521 --> 00:12:19,192
The price is wear and maintenance.

318
00:12:19,372 --> 00:12:20,625
Tracks are heavy,

319
00:12:20,625 --> 00:12:22,911
they can break or be thrown off

320
00:12:22,911 --> 00:12:24,017
in tight turns,

321
00:12:24,017 --> 00:12:26,523
and crews spend hours fixing them.

322
00:12:26,703 --> 00:12:29,275
But no wheeled vehicle can carry that

323
00:12:29,275 --> 00:12:31,986
much armor across that much bad ground.

324
00:12:32,335 --> 00:12:34,809
Why do some tanks carry small radar

325
00:12:34,809 --> 00:12:36,434
panels on their turret?

326
00:12:36,614 --> 00:12:39,458
Because they can shoot down incoming missiles.

327
00:12:39,638 --> 00:12:42,372
They are part of an active protection

328
00:12:42,372 --> 00:12:42,889
system.

329
00:12:43,069 --> 00:12:46,135
The radar detects a rocket or missile

330
00:12:46,135 --> 00:12:48,041
flying toward the tank,

331
00:12:48,041 --> 00:12:50,609
a computer calculates its path,

332
00:12:50,609 --> 00:12:53,344
and a launcher fires a burst that

333
00:12:53,344 --> 00:12:56,327
destroys or deflects it a few meters

334
00:12:56,327 --> 00:12:57,570
before it hits.

335
00:12:57,919 --> 00:12:59,892
Israel's Trophy system,

336
00:12:59,892 --> 00:13:01,866
mounted on the Merkava,

337
00:13:01,866 --> 00:13:05,383
made the first combat interception in two

338
00:13:05,383 --> 00:13:07,699
thousand eleven, near Gaza,

339
00:13:07,699 --> 00:13:10,272
stopping an anti tank missile.

340
00:13:10,452 --> 00:13:13,097
Since then, it has been fitted to

341
00:13:13,097 --> 00:13:16,703
some American Abrams and German Leopard tanks

342
00:13:16,703 --> 00:13:17,024
too.

343
00:13:17,204 --> 00:13:20,076
The whole sequence takes a fraction of

344
00:13:20,076 --> 00:13:20,756
a second.

345
00:13:21,106 --> 00:13:23,243
It is a big change in thinking.

346
00:13:23,423 --> 00:13:24,632
For a century,

347
00:13:24,632 --> 00:13:27,916
tanks survived by being hard to break.

348
00:13:28,096 --> 00:13:30,534
Now they also survive by not being

349
00:13:30,534 --> 00:13:31,323
hit at all.

350
00:13:31,503 --> 00:13:33,721
Slope, layers, bricks,

351
00:13:33,721 --> 00:13:35,333
cages and radar,

352
00:13:35,333 --> 00:13:39,062
every generation added a new layer of

353
00:13:39,062 --> 00:13:43,094
defense, and each one exists because the

354
00:13:43,094 --> 00:13:45,412
weapons got better too.

355
00:13:45,762 --> 00:13:47,783
So here are the eleven.

356
00:13:47,963 --> 00:13:49,836
A slanted front plate,

357
00:13:49,836 --> 00:13:51,709
a wedge on the turret,

358
00:13:51,709 --> 00:13:53,837
and secret layers inside.

359
00:13:54,017 --> 00:13:55,927
Bricks that explode,

360
00:13:55,927 --> 00:13:57,360
a cage of bars,

361
00:13:57,360 --> 00:14:00,416
and roof panels that blow off on

362
00:14:00,416 --> 00:14:01,181
purpose.

363
00:14:01,361 --> 00:14:03,267
An engine in the front,

364
00:14:03,267 --> 00:14:05,754
periscopes instead of windows,

365
00:14:05,754 --> 00:14:07,578
tubes that fire smoke,

366
00:14:07,578 --> 00:14:09,568
wide tracks for the mud,

367
00:14:09,568 --> 00:14:11,972
and a radar that shoots back.

368
00:14:12,322 --> 00:14:14,997
A tank looks like a simple block

369
00:14:14,997 --> 00:14:17,589
of steel, but every angle on it

370
00:14:17,589 --> 00:14:19,010
is a calculation.

371
00:14:19,190 --> 00:14:22,071
If you want more machines explained part

372
00:14:22,071 --> 00:14:24,809
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

373
00:14:24,989 --> 00:14:27,007
And tell us in the comments which

374
00:14:27,007 --> 00:14:29,025
machine you want us to take apart

375
00:14:29,025 --> 00:14:29,331
next.
